speechtext:
Agreed.
[Editor's note: The answer referred to above is as follows:]
The answer to part 1 is yes. As to part 2 the answer by services is as follows:
Air services, including casual and part time employees: December 31, 1957, 11.75 per cent; December 31, 1958, 9.07 per cent; December 31, 1959, 3.04 per cent.
Canal services, including casual and part time employees: December 31, 1957, .93 per cent (decrease); December 31, 1958, 2.94 per cent (decrease); December 31, 1959, 66.49 per cent (decrease).

Marine services, including casual and part time employees: December 31, 1957, 1.72 per cent; December 31, 1958, 5.7 per cent; December 31, 1959, 1.03 per cent.
With regard to part 3 I might say-(a) The development of formulae to measure realistically the work load foi a number of classes in the major work areas of the department.
(b)	The following delegation of authority which directly reduced work load, both at Ottawa headquarters and in regional and district offices:
Delegation of authority to regional and district offices for construction contracts to a value of $50,000 and maintenance contracts to a value of $5,000;
To approve and purchase locally non-stock materials, supplies and spare parts to a value of $1,000;
To rent and enter leases for departmental property;
To enter into service contracts in all cases where standard rates are applicable and no capital expenditures are involved;
Through decentralization to regional and district offices, accounting work for all operational and maintenance votes.
(c)	The more important operational changes which slowed up growth or reduced staff are the following:
Electrification of lighthouses at many locations;
Replacement of older ships with modem vessels capable of greater work capacity and effectiveness;
Discontinuance of lifesaving service at Sable island;
Introduction of high speed mobile equipment for runway clearing;
Establishment of more formalized procedures for program and co-ordination of planning in relation to construction programs;
Introduction of a 54-hour work week at northern stations;
Installation of automatic telephone system for Magdalen island;
Transfer to provide private ownership of telephone and communications systems in the maritime provinces, Alberta and Saskatchewan;
Reducing the number of inspections required for certain licensed radio stations;
Use of contract cleaning service at airport terminal buildings.
(d)	For the purpose of obtaining greater flexibility, improved analyses and to slow up rate of growth, the department has developed a program whereby a substantial number of projects are now undertaken on data processing machines.
In answer to part 4, consideration has taken the following forms:
Orders for Returns
Group discussions at district, regional and headquarters levels.
Special teams studying specific problems, either within region or at headquarters.
Discussions with outside commercial organizations having similar experience and problems.
Use of suggestions received in suggestion award program.
Training programs directed toward establishing good supervisory practices and high employee productivity and employment.
Use of special departmental committees to make special annual review of staff requirements and to thoroughly screen requests for additional staff. Also an annual review of department staff by an establishment review committee consisting of officers of the civil service commission, treasury board and the department.
It is considered that staff increases would have been substantially greater had not the procedures and actions outlined above been taken.
